C++ syntax is similar to C, but with added features for OOP. Key elements include: `int`, `float`, `double`, `char`, `bool` are fundamental data types. Variables are declared with a type and name (e.g., `int age;`). Operators perform operations on data (e.g., `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `=`). Control flow statements (`if`, `else`, `for`, `while`) control the execution of code based on conditions. Functions are blocks of code that perform specific tasks. Input/output operations are handled using `std::cin` (input) and `std::cout` (output). Comments are used to explain code (`// single-line`, `/* multi-line */`).

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  int age = 30;
  std::cout << "Age: " << age << std::endl;
  return 0;
}
```

A class is a blueprint for creating objects. It defines the attributes (data members) and behaviors (member functions) that objects of that class will have. An object is an instance of a class.  To define a class, use the `class` keyword. Member functions are defined within the class. Objects are created using the class name followed by the object name (e.g., `MyClass obj;`).  Access members of an object using the dot operator (`.`).

```cpp
class Dog {
public:
  std::string breed;
  int age;
  void bark() {
    std::cout << "Woof!" << std::endl;
  }
};

int main() {
  Dog myDog;
  myDog.breed = "Labrador";
  myDog.age = 3;
  myDog.bark();
  return 0;
}
```

A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable. Dynamic memory allocation allows you to allocate memory during runtime. `new` operator is used to allocate memory and `delete` operator is used to deallocate memory. Pointers are used to access and manipulate data stored in dynamically allocated memory. Proper memory management is crucial to prevent memory leaks. Memory leaks occur when dynamically allocated memory is not deallocated after it is no longer needed.

```cpp
int *ptr = new int;
*ptr = 10;
std::cout << *ptr << std::endl;
delete ptr;
ptr = nullptr;
```
